# The national unit for britain. Items take n=0; the world items reviewed here change by level.
# Dates are in engine/notation/chronicle.ts with their sources.
lesson history-g4-britain-pages-of-our-history v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=9 subject=history nation=britain levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Roman Britain"
  goal "Put the story of Roman Britain in order, count its years across year one, and say why the Romans built Hadrian's Wall."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=-100 to=150 step=50 years=[-55, 43, 122] labels=["Caesar lands in Britain", "Romans conquer Britain", "Hadrian's Wall begun"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      Julius Caesar landed in Britain in 55 BC, but the Romans conquered it only from AD 43. They built roads, towns and baths, and a fort at Vindolanda, where letters written on thin wooden tablets have been found. In AD 122 the Emperor Hadrian had a wall begun across the north of Britain, from sea to sea.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.n4-what n=0
    }
    show history.n4-order n=0 k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.n4-between n=0 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.n4-century n=0 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.source-hand k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.bc-ad-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.money-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.n4-order n=0 k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=0
    level medium hard {
      show history.n4-what n=0
    }
    level easy {
      show history.n4-century n=0 k=1
    }
    level hard {
      show history.source-hand k=5
    }
  }
  story {
    show history.n4-between n=0 k=1 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.n4-why n=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.n4-find-out n=0
  }
  remember {
    say "Every date in our country's story can be put on the line and counted from, and every one came from a source."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is to use the grade's methods (timelines across year one, centuries, sources) on a
    chapter of the family's own country. The way in names what a thing was; the core orders three
    events, counts the years between two, names a century, and reviews sources, BC and AD and the
    story of money; the two-star question asks why something was done. If the child is stuck on
    counting, count to the next hundred first. The three-star task is for you to mark: a small
    piece of finding out with you. England's key stage 2 history asks for "the Roman Empire and its impact on Britain", naming "Julius Caesar's attempted invasion in 55-54 BC" and "the Roman Empire by AD 42 and the power of its army" among its examples, and "Hadrian's Wall" among the things built.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example says what the thing was, the events come nearly in order, the years counted are the closest pair, and the review questions are the plainest."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the events are fully muddled, the years counted are the longer pair, the century asked has a trap, and the review questions cross year one."
  }
}
